window.META_MATH = {"subject": "Mathematical Logic and Set Theory", "projects": [{"id": "LF-01", "slug": "cantor-theorem", "title": "Cantor’s Theorem and the Power-Set Hierarchy", "level": "Introductory", "idea": "<p>For any proposed enumeration of subsets, choose a new subset that\ndisagrees with the assigned subset at each element’s own membership\ntest.</p>", "target": "<p>For every set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nprove there is no surjection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the set of all subsets of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>↦</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\mapsto\\{a\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an injection, conclude that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas strictly smaller cardinality than its power set, in the sense of\ninjection plus absence of a bijection. The diagonal argument does not\nrequire the axiom of choice.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use the power-set and separation axioms of ordinary set theory, the\ndefinitions of injective, surjective, and bijective functions, and\nclassical propositional logic. Separation permits forming a subset of an\nalready given set by a formula. No unrestricted “set of all sets”\ncomprehension is used.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Construct the diagonal subset", "role": "<p>Use bounded separation to reverse each element’s own membership\ndecision. <strong>Local obligation:</strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\{a\\in A:a\\notin f(a)\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a well-defined member of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>An arbitrary function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "exportId": "E1", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\{a\\in A:a\\notin f(a)\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a well-defined member of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>The definition produces a subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso it belongs to the codomain; no self-containing universal set is\nneeded.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the construction is made over all sets rather than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nrestrict it to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>;\nbounded separation is the actual permitted axiom. Withhold this export\nand recheck its descendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>For each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the membership statement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis meaningful. By separation, the elements of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor which this statement is false form a set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\subset A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>The definition gives, for each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe exact equivalence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in D\\iff a\\notin f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIn particular\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>\nis in the stated codomain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E1/v1 only after establishing this obligation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\{a\\in A:a\\notin f(a)\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a well-defined member of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>An arbitrary function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nRetain the stated assumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use bounded separation to reverse each element’s own membership\ndecision.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The definition produces a subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso it belongs to the codomain; no self-containing universal set is\nneeded.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N1.1–N1.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Exclude every preimage", "role": "<p>Specialize the membership equivalence at the alleged preimage and\nderive a contradiction. <strong>Local obligation:</strong> No\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis surjective; the singleton map establishes strict cardinal growth.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1 and a hypothetical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)=D</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1.</p>", "exportId": "E2", "export": "<p>No\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis surjective; the singleton map establishes strict cardinal growth.</p>", "gate": "<p>The contradiction applies to every proposed surjection, including\nfinite and empty domains.</p>", "repair": "<p>If a contradiction is interpreted as showing the power set is\ninconsistent, identify the rejected assumption: only the proposed\nsurjectivity fails. Withhold this export and recheck its descendants\nafter a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)=D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe equivalence in E1 reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in D\\iff b\\notin D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nimpossible in classical logic. Thus no element of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nmaps to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand the arbitrary function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nis not surjective.</p>", "<p>The singleton map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>↦</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\mapsto\\{a\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis injective because equal singleton sets have equal elements. A\nbijection would in particular be a surjection, already ruled out. This\nproves the claimed strict cardinal comparison. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>⌀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)=\\{\\varnothing\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand the same lack of surjection is immediate.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E2/v1 only after establishing this obligation: No\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis surjective; the singleton map establishes strict cardinal growth.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1 and a hypothetical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)=D</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Specialize the membership equivalence at the alleged preimage and\nderive a contradiction.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The contradiction applies to every proposed surjection, including\nfinite and empty domains.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N2.1–N2.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}], "edges": [{"id": "T1-2", "source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N2.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 and a hypothetical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)=D</annotation></semantics></math>.\nCheck: The contradiction applies to every proposed surjection, including\nfinite and empty domains.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-construct-the-diagonal-subset-1\">N1 — Construct the diagonal\nsubset</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N1.1.</strong> For each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso the membership statement\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis meaningful. By separation, the elements of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor which this statement is false form a set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\subset A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N1.2.</strong> The definition gives, for each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe exact equivalence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in D\\iff a\\notin f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIn particular\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>\nis in the stated codomain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-exclude-every-preimage-1\">N2 — Exclude every preimage</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N2.1.</strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(b)=D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe equivalence in E1 reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in D\\iff b\\notin D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nimpossible in classical logic. Thus no element of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nmaps to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand the arbitrary function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\nis not surjective.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N2.2.</strong> The singleton map\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>↦</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\mapsto\\{a\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis injective because equal singleton sets have equal elements. A\nbijection would in particular be a surjection, already ruled out. This\nproves the claimed strict cardinal comparison. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>⌀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>⌀</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(A)=\\{\\varnothing\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand the same lack of surjection is immediate.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\{1,2,3\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nconsider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(1)=\\{1,2\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(2)=\\{1\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(3)=\\{3\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe diagonal rule includes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2</annotation></semantics></math>\nbut excludes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\{2\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIt is different from all three listed images, illustrating the exact\nself-index disagreement.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>Listing all finite subsets of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb N</annotation></semantics></math>\nis possible and does not contradict Cantor: the codomain is then only a\nproper part of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ℕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(\\mathbb N)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe diagonal subset for such a list cannot be one of its listed finite\nsubsets. Replacing “all subsets” by “finite subsets” changes the\ntarget.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Rejected argument, diagnosis, and correction", "html": "<p><strong>Rejected scope (N1):</strong> form the set of all sets that\ndo not contain themselves. <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> Memory replaced\nseparation over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nby unrestricted comprehension. <strong>Repair:</strong> construct only\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\subset A</annotation></semantics></math>\nusing the given map; its existence is licensed by separation. Then N2\ncontradicts surjectivity, not the set axioms. Recheck the codomain\nmembership and final cardinal comparison.</p>"}, {"label": "Publication and reuse", "html": "<p>The corrected export retains its explicit assumptions. A hypothetical\nrevision receives a new version, and each consuming activity is\nrechecked; this scenario is not a recorded runtime event. <strong>Next\ndirection:</strong> Apply the construction to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\mathbb N</annotation></semantics></math>\nto obtain an uncountable power set and to explain why no single list can\nenumerate all binary sequences.</p>"}], "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes_4.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Set Theory notes</a> for sets and ultraproducts; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~antonio/math125A/mathlogicP1.pdf\">Berkeley,\nMathematical Logic for Mathematicians, Part I</a> for first-order\nsemantics; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/computability_notes_v1.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Computability Theory notes</a> for effective procedures and\nundecidability. The proofs below specify their own set-theoretic and\nsemantic assumptions.</p>", "sha256": "85f9f9b444cf7e7d21cc72010fc29740e46cee964e78a54b59127172d3b97609", "pdf": "reports/pdf/cantor-theorem.pdf"}, {"id": "LF-02", "slug": "cantor-schroeder-bernstein", "title": "Cantor–Schröder–Bernstein Theorem", "level": "Intermediate", "idea": "<p>Start chains at the part of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nnot hit by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>.\nFollow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\circ f</annotation></semantics></math>\nforward from those starts; use\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f</annotation></semantics></math>\non that chain region and reverse\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>\neverywhere else.</p>", "target": "<p>If injections\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:A\\to B</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g:B\\to A</annotation></semantics></math>\nexist, prove there is a bijection\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h:A\\to B</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe construction works for arbitrary sets and does not need a choice of\npreimages beyond the unique preimages already supplied by an\ninjection.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use ordinary set operations, recursion over\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℕ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb N</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand injectivity. The notation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g^{-1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelow refers to the unique preimage of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nit is not defined on all of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>.\nNo cardinal arithmetic or axiom of choice is needed for the proof.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Separate the forward-chain region", "role": "<p>Identify exactly which elements of the chain region lie in the image\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>.\n<strong>Local obligation:</strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=g(f(C))</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\setminus C\\subset g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=A\\setminus g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_{n+1}=g(f(A_n))</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "exportId": "E1", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=g(f(C))</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\setminus C\\subset g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Every point outside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>\nis in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0\\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "repair": "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis used outside its image, first prove the complement inclusion; it is\nwhat makes the second branch legitimate. Withhold this export and\nrecheck its descendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwhereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0</annotation></semantics></math>\nis disjoint from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>Images commute with unions, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(C))=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}g(f(A_n))=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis proves the equality. Also if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>,\nit cannot belong to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=A\\setminus g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso it lies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E1/v1 only after establishing this obligation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=g(f(C))</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\setminus C\\subset g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=A\\setminus g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_{n+1}=g(f(A_n))</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nRetain the stated assumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Identify exactly which elements of the chain region lie in the image\nof\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Every point outside\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>\nis in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0\\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N1.1–N1.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Define and prove the bijection", "role": "<p>Prove the two branch images are disjoint and together exhaust\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>.\n<strong>Local obligation:</strong> The piecewise-defined\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h:A\\to B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a bijection.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(a)=f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in C</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(a)=g^{-1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1.</p>", "exportId": "E2", "export": "<p>The piecewise-defined\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h:A\\to B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a bijection.</p>", "gate": "<p>Injectivity on each branch alone does not rule out collisions across\nbranches.</p>", "repair": "<p>If surjectivity is asserted from the two original injections,\nexplicitly split\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math>\naccording to membership in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand use E1 to identify its branch. Withhold this export and recheck its\ndescendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>E1 makes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math>\nwell defined. Each branch is injective. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in C</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>\nhad\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)=g^{-1}(a&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;=g(f(a))\\in g(f(C))\\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>,\na contradiction. Thus cross-branch collisions are impossible.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nit is hit by the first branch. Otherwise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(b)\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>:\nif it were in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=g(f(C))</annotation></semantics></math>,\ninjectivity of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>\nwould force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(b)\\in A\\setminus C</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand the second branch maps it to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis proves surjectivity and hence bijectivity.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E2/v1 only after establishing this obligation: The\npiecewise-defined\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h:A\\to B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a bijection.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(a)=f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in C</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(a)=g^{-1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Prove the two branch images are disjoint and together exhaust\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Injectivity on each branch alone does not rule out collisions across\nbranches.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N2.1–N2.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}], "edges": [{"id": "T1-2", "source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N2.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(a)=f(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in C</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(a)=g^{-1}(a)</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>.\nCheck: Injectivity on each branch alone does not rule out collisions\nacross branches.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-separate-the-forward-chain-region-1\">N1 — Separate the\nforward-chain region</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N1.1.</strong> Every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwhereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0</annotation></semantics></math>\nis disjoint from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nHence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N1.2.</strong> Images commute with unions, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(f(C))=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}g(f(A_n))=\\bigcup_{n\\ge1}A_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis proves the equality. Also if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>,\nit cannot belong to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=A\\setminus g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso it lies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-define-and-prove-the-bijection-1\">N2 — Define and prove the\nbijection</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N2.1.</strong> E1 makes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math>\nwell defined. Each branch is injective. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\in C</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>\nhad\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>−</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a)=g^{-1}(a&#39;)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;=g(f(a))\\in g(f(C))\\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>,\na contradiction. Thus cross-branch collisions are impossible.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N2.2.</strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>\nit is hit by the first branch. Otherwise\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∉</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(b)\\notin C</annotation></semantics></math>:\nif it were in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C\\cap g(B)=g(f(C))</annotation></semantics></math>,\ninjectivity of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>\nwould force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b\\in f(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(b)\\in A\\setminus C</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand the second branch maps it to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis proves surjectivity and hence bijectivity.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>Take\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=B=\\mathbb N_0</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(n)=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(n)=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=\\{0\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_n=\\{2n\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math>\nis the set of even numbers. The constructed bijection sends even\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand odd\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>\nto\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>,\nswapping every adjacent pair. This verifies both branches and their\ndisjoint images.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>A piecewise choice of two injective rules can fail globally: on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{0,1\\}</annotation></semantics></math>,\nassign\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\mapsto0</annotation></semantics></math>\non the first singleton branch and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\mapsto0</annotation></semantics></math>\non the second. Each restricted map is injective, but the combined map is\nnot. The cross-branch argument above is therefore an essential proof\nobligation.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Rejected argument, diagnosis, and correction", "html": "<p><strong>Rejected N2 proof:</strong> both pieces are injective, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h</annotation></semantics></math>\nis injective. <strong>Sense:</strong> the two-singleton example isolates\nthe missing cross-case. <strong>Repair:</strong> assume a cross\ncollision and apply\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g</annotation></semantics></math>\nto derive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>⊂</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a&#39;\\in g(f(C))\\subset C</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncontradicting its branch membership. This fills the gap with no change\nof assumptions; recheck bijectivity after surjectivity is also\nestablished.</p>"}, {"label": "Publication and reuse", "html": "<p>The corrected export retains its explicit assumptions. A hypothetical\nrevision receives a new version, and each consuming activity is\nrechecked; this scenario is not a recorded runtime event. <strong>Next\ndirection:</strong> Use explicit injections to compare infinite\nstructures while retaining this construction as the justification for\nturning two inequalities into a bijection.</p>"}], "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes_4.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Set Theory notes</a> for sets and ultraproducts; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~antonio/math125A/mathlogicP1.pdf\">Berkeley,\nMathematical Logic for Mathematicians, Part I</a> for first-order\nsemantics; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/computability_notes_v1.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Computability Theory notes</a> for effective procedures and\nundecidability. The proofs below specify their own set-theoretic and\nsemantic assumptions.</p>", "sha256": "5646440e7e4fcb95fa8347d52369fff819f61261d16b6505a1422f76a379e708", "pdf": "reports/pdf/cantor-schroeder-bernstein.pdf"}, {"id": "LF-03", "slug": "first-order-compactness-ultraproduct", "title": "First-Order Compactness via Ultraproducts", "level": "Advanced", "idea": "<p>Each finite fragment gets its own model. An ultrafilter regards all\nfragments containing any chosen sentence as large; the quotient product\nthen makes every sentence in the theory true at once.</p>", "target": "<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe a set of sentences in a first-order language\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith finite-arity relation and function symbols and nonempty structures.\nIf every finite subset of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a model, prove\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a model. Work in classical ZFC set theory, including choice. The\nproof constructs an ultraproduct and proves the required truth-transfer\nlemma rather than importing first-order completeness.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use Zorn’s lemma, choice of structures and witnesses from set-indexed\nnonempty families, finite-intersection filters, and the recursive\nsemantics of first-order formulas. A proper filter on an index set\ncontains the whole set, not the empty set, and is closed under finite\nintersections and supersets. An ultrafilter is a maximal proper filter.\nFunction symbols may include zero-arity constants. General formulas can\nbe expressed using atomic formulas, negation, conjunction, and\nexistential quantification.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Build a fine ultrafilter", "role": "<p>Extend the filter generated by the cones to an ultrafilter and derive\nits complement decision property. <strong>Local obligation:</strong>\nThere is an ultrafilter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand exactly one of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,I\\setminus A</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> finite</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\{s\\subset T:s\\text{ finite}\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi=\\{s\\in I:\\varphi\\in s\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "exportId": "E1", "export": "<p>There is an ultrafilter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand exactly one of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,I\\setminus A</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Every finite intersection of cones contains their finite list of\nsentences as an index, so it is nonempty.</p>", "repair": "<p>If maximality is used without a chain upper bound, supply the\nunion-of-chain argument and check that the empty set never enters.\nWithhold this export and recheck its descendants after a versioned\ncorrection.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Finite intersections of the cones are nonempty: the finite union of\nthe required sentences is itself an index. They therefore generate a\nproper filter (if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>\nis empty use the filter containing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>).\nThe union of a chain of proper filters is again a proper filter, because\nany finitely many of its members occur together in one member of the\nchain. Zorn supplies a maximal proper filter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\notin\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>,\nadjoining it cannot generate a proper larger filter. Thus some\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>⌀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\cap A=\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>;\notherwise its intersections with old filter members would give a proper\nextension. Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\setminus A\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.\nBoth complementary sets cannot belong, since their intersection is\nempty. This is the required decision property.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E1/v1 only after establishing this obligation: There is an\nultrafilter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand exactly one of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,I\\setminus A</annotation></semantics></math>\nbelongs to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset I</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> finite</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\{s\\subset T:s\\text{ finite}\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi=\\{s\\in I:\\varphi\\in s\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.\nRetain the stated assumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Extend the filter generated by the cones to an ultrafilter and derive\nits complement decision property.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Every finite intersection of cones contains their finite list of\nsentences as an index, so it is nonempty.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N1.1–N1.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Construct the quotient structure", "role": "<p>Quotient the product by agreement on an ultrafilter-large set and\ndefine symbols coordinatewise. <strong>Local obligation:</strong> The\nproduct modulo\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sim b\\iff\\{s:a_s=b_s\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a well-defined nonempty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>-structure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1; choose a nonempty model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s\\models s</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1.</p>", "exportId": "E2", "export": "<p>The product modulo\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sim b\\iff\\{s:a_s=b_s\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a well-defined nonempty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>-structure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Finite intersections, not countable intersections, suffice for each\nfinite-arity symbol; representatives must not affect truth or function\nvalues.</p>", "repair": "<p>If infinitely many representative agreements are intersected for one\nformula, use only its finite tuple of free variables and the finite\narities actually present. Withhold this export and recheck its\ndescendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Choice gives the family of models and an element in each domain, so\ntheir product is nonempty. Agreement is reflexive and symmetric;\ntransitivity follows because the intersection of two large agreement\nsets is large and is contained in the third agreement set. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an equivalence relation.</p>", "<p>Interpret a function on classes by applying it in each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand interpret a relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R([a^1],\\ldots,[a^r])</annotation></semantics></math>\nas true when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models R(a_s^1,\\ldots,a_s^r)\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIf representatives change, they agree on the finite intersection of\ntheir large agreement sets. Function outputs and relation truth agree\nthere, so their quotient value or largeness is unchanged. Equality is\nthe equivalence relation itself; constants are their coordinatewise\nclasses. This defines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E2/v1 only after establishing this obligation: The product\nmodulo\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>⇔</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a\\sim b\\iff\\{s:a_s=b_s\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a well-defined nonempty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>-structure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1; choose a nonempty model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s\\models s</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Quotient the product by agreement on an ultrafilter-large set and\ndefine symbols coordinatewise.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Finite intersections, not countable intersections, suffice for each\nfinite-arity symbol; representatives must not affect truth or function\nvalues.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N2.1–N2.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Prove truth transfer", "role": "<p>Induct on terms and formula construction; use chosen witnesses for\nthe existential direction. <strong>Local obligation:</strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\theta([a^1],\\ldots,[a^r])</annotation></semantics></math>\niff\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models\\theta(a_s^1,\\ldots,a_s^r)\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1 and E2/v1, a first-order formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand product representatives for its finitely many parameters. Incoming\nproof exports: E1/v1 at N3.1, E2/v1 at N3.1.</p>", "exportId": "E3", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\theta([a^1],\\ldots,[a^r])</annotation></semantics></math>\niff\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models\\theta(a_s^1,\\ldots,a_s^r)\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Existential witnesses may vary with the index. Requiring one common\nelement of all models would be an invalid strengthening.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the existential case is dismissed as automatic, construct the\ncoordinatewise witnesses and explicitly use the induction hypothesis for\nthe matrix formula. Withhold this export and recheck its descendants\nafter a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Induction on terms shows evaluation is coordinatewise up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>:\nvariables and constants have this property by definition, and applying a\nfunction preserves it. Atomic equality and relations therefore satisfy\nthe asserted transfer by N2. Negation corresponds to complement, handled\nby the ultrafilter decision property. Conjunction corresponds to\nintersection; a finite intersection is large exactly when both factors\nare large, by filter closure and upward closure.</p>", "<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃</mo><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists z\\,\\psi(z,\\bar a)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nfirst suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a witnessing class\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[b]</annotation></semantics></math>.\nBy the induction hypothesis,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models\\psi(b_s,\\bar a_s)\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis large. It is contained in the set of indices satisfying the\nexistential formula, so that set is large.</p>", "<p>Conversely, if the existential satisfaction set is large, choose a\nwitness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nin each of its models, and choose an arbitrary domain element at the\nremaining indices. Choice and nonempty domains permit this product\nelement. The set where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(b_s,\\bar a_s)</annotation></semantics></math>\nholds contains the large existential set. The induction hypothesis then\nmakes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[b]</annotation></semantics></math>\na witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThese cases complete the structural induction and prove truth\ntransfer.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E3/v1 only after establishing this obligation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\theta([a^1],\\ldots,[a^r])</annotation></semantics></math>\niff\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models\\theta(a_s^1,\\ldots,a_s^r)\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1 and E2/v1, a first-order formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand product representatives for its finitely many parameters. Incoming\nproof exports: E1/v1 at N3.1, E2/v1 at N3.1. Retain the stated\nassumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Induct on terms and formula construction; use chosen witnesses for\nthe existential direction.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Existential witnesses may vary with the index. Requiring one common\nelement of all models would be an invalid strengthening.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N3.1–N3.3 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N4", "title": "Assemble a model of the whole theory", "role": "<p>Use each sentence’s cone as a subset of its satisfaction set.\n<strong>Local obligation:</strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models T</annotation></semantics></math>,\nproving compactness.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1 cones and E3/v1 truth transfer for sentences. Incoming proof\nexports: E1/v1 at N4.1, E3/v1 at N4.2.</p>", "exportId": "E4", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models T</annotation></semantics></math>,\nproving compactness.</p>", "gate": "<p>The final assertion quantifies over every sentence separately; no\ninfinite intersection in the ultrafilter is required.</p>", "repair": "<p>If all cones are intersected and assumed large, replace that step by\none-sentence-at-a-time truth transfer; filters are only finitely\nintersection-closed. Withhold this export and recheck its descendants\nafter a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Fix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.\nWhenever\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in C_\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe chosen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a model of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso it satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi\\subset\\{s:M_s\\models\\varphi\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe left side is large by N1, hence so is the right side.</p>", "<p>By N3,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.\nSince this holds for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\in T</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models T</annotation></semantics></math>.\nNo common index need satisfy all of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe quotient construction is what supplies the new model.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E4/v1 only after establishing this obligation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models T</annotation></semantics></math>,\nproving compactness.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1 cones and E3/v1 truth transfer for sentences. Incoming proof\nexports: E1/v1 at N4.1, E3/v1 at N4.2. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use each sentence’s cone as a subset of its satisfaction set.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The final assertion quantifies over every sentence separately; no\ninfinite intersection in the ultrafilter is required.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N4.1–N4.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}], "edges": [{"id": "T1-2", "source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N2.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1; choose a nonempty model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s\\models s</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in I</annotation></semantics></math>.\nCheck: Finite intersections, not countable intersections, suffice for\neach finite-arity symbol; representatives must not affect truth or\nfunction values.</p>"}, {"id": "T1-3", "source": "N1", "target": "N3", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N3.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 and E2/v1, a first-order formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand product representatives for its finitely many parameters. Check:\nExistential witnesses may vary with the index. Requiring one common\nelement of all models would be an invalid strengthening.</p>"}, {"id": "T2-3", "source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "activity": "N3.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 and E2/v1, a first-order formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand product representatives for its finitely many parameters. Check:\nExistential witnesses may vary with the index. Requiring one common\nelement of all models would be an invalid strengthening.</p>"}, {"id": "T1-4", "source": "N1", "target": "N4", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N4.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 cones and E3/v1 truth transfer for sentences. Check: The final\nassertion quantifies over every sentence separately; no infinite\nintersection in the ultrafilter is required.</p>"}, {"id": "T3-4", "source": "N3", "target": "N4", "export": "E3/v1", "activity": "N4.2", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 cones and E3/v1 truth transfer for sentences. Check: The final\nassertion quantifies over every sentence separately; no infinite\nintersection in the ultrafilter is required.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-build-a-fine-ultrafilter-1\">N1 — Build a fine\nultrafilter</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N1.1.</strong> Finite intersections of the cones are\nnonempty: the finite union of the required sentences is itself an index.\nThey therefore generate a proper filter (if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>\nis empty use the filter containing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I</annotation></semantics></math>).\nThe union of a chain of proper filters is again a proper filter, because\nany finitely many of its members occur together in one member of the\nchain. Zorn supplies a maximal proper filter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>𝒰</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N1.2.</strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\notin\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>,\nadjoining it cannot generate a proper larger filter. Thus some\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>⌀</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\cap A=\\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>;\notherwise its intersections with old filter members would give a proper\nextension. Therefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>\\</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I\\setminus A\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.\nBoth complementary sets cannot belong, since their intersection is\nempty. This is the required decision property.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-construct-the-quotient-structure-1\">N2 — Construct the\nquotient structure</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N2.1.</strong> Choice gives the family of models and an\nelement in each domain, so their product is nonempty. Agreement is\nreflexive and symmetric; transitivity follows because the intersection\nof two large agreement sets is large and is contained in the third\nagreement set. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>\nis an equivalence relation.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N2.2.</strong> Interpret a function on classes by\napplying it in each\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand interpret a relation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R([a^1],\\ldots,[a^r])</annotation></semantics></math>\nas true when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝒰</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models R(a_s^1,\\ldots,a_s^r)\\}\\in\\mathcal U</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIf representatives change, they agree on the finite intersection of\ntheir large agreement sets. Function outputs and relation truth agree\nthere, so their quotient value or largeness is unchanged. Equality is\nthe equivalence relation itself; constants are their coordinatewise\nclasses. This defines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-prove-truth-transfer-1\">N3 — Prove truth transfer</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N3.1.</strong> Induction on terms shows evaluation is\ncoordinatewise up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sim</annotation></semantics></math>:\nvariables and constants have this property by definition, and applying a\nfunction preserves it. Atomic equality and relations therefore satisfy\nthe asserted transfer by N2. Negation corresponds to complement, handled\nby the ultrafilter decision property. Conjunction corresponds to\nintersection; a finite intersection is large exactly when both factors\nare large, by filter closure and upward closure.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N3.2.</strong> For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃</mo><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists z\\,\\psi(z,\\bar a)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nfirst suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a witnessing class\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[b]</annotation></semantics></math>.\nBy the induction hypothesis,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{s:M_s\\models\\psi(b_s,\\bar a_s)\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\nis large. It is contained in the set of indices satisfying the\nexistential formula, so that set is large.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N3.3.</strong> Conversely, if the existential\nsatisfaction set is large, choose a witness\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nin each of its models, and choose an arbitrary domain element at the\nremaining indices. Choice and nonempty domains permit this product\nelement. The set where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover><mi>a</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\psi(b_s,\\bar a_s)</annotation></semantics></math>\nholds contains the large existential set. The induction hypothesis then\nmakes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[b]</annotation></semantics></math>\na witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThese cases complete the structural induction and prove truth\ntransfer.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n4-assemble-a-model-of-the-whole-theory-1\">N4 — Assemble a model\nof the whole theory</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N4.1.</strong> Fix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\in T</annotation></semantics></math>.\nWhenever\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\in C_\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>,\nthe chosen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M_s</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a model of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso it satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.\nTherefore\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>⊨</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_\\varphi\\subset\\{s:M_s\\models\\varphi\\}</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe left side is large by N1, hence so is the right side.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N4.2.</strong> By N3,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\varphi</annotation></semantics></math>.\nSince this holds for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\in T</annotation></semantics></math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models T</annotation></semantics></math>.\nNo common index need satisfy all of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe quotient construction is what supplies the new model.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>In the language of ordered fields, add a constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>\nand all sentences\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><munder><munder><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo accent=\"true\">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mi>n</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.333em\"></mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\"> terms</mtext></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c&gt;\\underbrace{1+\\cdots+1}_{n\\text{ terms}}</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n\\ge1</annotation></semantics></math>,\ntogether with the ordered-field axioms. Every finite fragment has a\nmodel in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math>\nby choosing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c</annotation></semantics></math>\nlarger than the finitely many listed integers. Compactness gives an\nordered field with an element larger than every standard positive\ninteger. This is a non-Archimedean model, not an infinitely large real\nnumber.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>Compactness fails if one insists that the resulting model be finite.\nThe sentences “there are at least\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n</annotation></semantics></math>\ndistinct elements” have every finite fragment satisfiable in a finite\nset, but no single finite model satisfies them all. The theorem permits\narbitrary nonempty models.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Rejected argument, diagnosis, and correction", "html": "<p><strong>Rejected N4 argument:</strong> intersect all cones and choose\none finite-fragment model satisfying everything. For the\nincreasing-cardinality example that intersection contains no suitable\nfinite index. <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> Intellect used infinite\nintersection closure that an ultrafilter need not possess.\n<strong>Repair:</strong> form the quotient product and use the proven\ntruth-transfer lemma separately for each sentence. The original\nfirst-order compactness theorem is unchanged; recheck every sentence via\nits own cone.</p>"}, {"label": "Publication and reuse", "html": "<p>The corrected export retains its explicit assumptions. A hypothetical\nrevision receives a new version, and each consuming activity is\nrechecked; this scenario is not a recorded runtime event. <strong>Next\ndirection:</strong> Use compactness to build models with prescribed\nfinite consistency patterns, keeping nonstandard objects distinct from\nobjects in the original structure.</p>"}], "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes_4.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Set Theory notes</a> for sets and ultraproducts; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~antonio/math125A/mathlogicP1.pdf\">Berkeley,\nMathematical Logic for Mathematicians, Part I</a> for first-order\nsemantics; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/computability_notes_v1.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Computability Theory notes</a> for effective procedures and\nundecidability. The proofs below specify their own set-theoretic and\nsemantic assumptions.</p>", "sha256": "eeffde339dd0fc228961f6afd9903474a9e4015d42badb611e84b59f9a08ebc3", "pdf": "reports/pdf/first-order-compactness-ultraproduct.pdf"}, {"id": "LF-04", "slug": "downward-lowenheim-skolem", "title": "Downward Löwenheim–Skolem for Countable Languages", "level": "Advanced", "idea": "<p>Close a countable seed not only under functions, but also under\nwitnesses for every existential statement with existing parameters.\nRepeat countably many times so newly added parameters receive their\nwitnesses too.</p>", "target": "<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>\nbe a countable first-order language with finite-arity symbols,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\na nonempty\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>-structure,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\subset M</annotation></semantics></math>\nat most countable. Prove there is an at most countable elementary\nsubstructure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\preccurlyeq M</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>.\nElementary means every first-order formula with parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas the same truth value in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIf\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\ncan and must in this construction be countably infinite. Work in\nclassical set theory with countable choices of witnesses.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use countability of finite strings over a countable alphabet and of\nfinite Cartesian powers of a countable set, and countable unions of\ncountable sets. Interpret functions and relations of a substructure by\nrestriction. The existential witness criterion for elementarity is\nproved here by formula induction; it is not imported as an unchecked\ntheorem.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Build the witness closure", "role": "<p>At each stage add function values and one witness for each true\nexistential formula over the current parameter set. <strong>Local\nobligation:</strong> There are increasing countable sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontains all function values on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nand witnesses for all true existential formulas with parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>A countable seed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊃</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0\\supset A</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining one element of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand all formulas and language symbols.</p>", "exportId": "E1", "export": "<p>There are increasing countable sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontains all function values on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nand witnesses for all true existential formulas with parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Each stage makes only countably many requests; parameters added later\nrequire new stages.</p>", "repair": "<p>If closure is performed just once, repeat it through all\nnatural-number stages so formulas involving newly added witnesses are\ncovered. Withhold this export and recheck its descendants after a\nversioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Choose one element of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nand adjoin it to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nto make\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0</annotation></semantics></math>\nnonempty. Given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\ninclude in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nthe interpretations of all constants and of all finite-arity function\nsymbols applied to tuples from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThere are countably many such requests.</p>", "<p>Also list every existential formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\,\\psi(x,\\bar b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith a finite tuple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar b</annotation></semantics></math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nthat is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThere are countably many formulas and parameter tuples. Choose one\nwitness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each true request and add it. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nitself included, the result remains countable and the construction can\nbe repeated.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E1/v1 only after establishing this obligation: There are\nincreasing countable sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontains all function values on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nand witnesses for all true existential formulas with parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>A countable seed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊃</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0\\supset A</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining one element of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand all formulas and language symbols. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>At each stage add function values and one witness for each true\nexistential formula over the current parameter set.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Each stage makes only countably many requests; parameters added later\nrequire new stages.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N1.1–N1.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Obtain a countable substructure with witnesses", "role": "<p>Use finiteness of parameter tuples to locate all their entries in one\nstage. <strong>Local obligation:</strong> The restricted structure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis countable, contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand every existential formula true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1.</p>", "exportId": "E2", "export": "<p>The restricted structure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis countable, contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand every existential formula true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Closure under functions alone is insufficient for elementarity;\nretain the existential witness closure as a distinct export.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the proof assumes the union is closed under an operation with\ninfinitely many arguments, restore the finite-arity language contract;\nthe common-stage argument depends on it. Withhold this export and\nrecheck its descendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>The union\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis nonempty and at most countable. Any finite tuple from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies entirely in some\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso each language function evaluated there lies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}\\subset B</annotation></semantics></math>.\nConstants also lie in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus restriction defines an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>-substructure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mo>∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\exists x\\,\\psi(x,\\bar b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar b</annotation></semantics></math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nchoose a stage containing the whole finite tuple. N1 added a witness at\nthe next stage. Hence such a witness is in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith the matrix formula true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis is the precise witness property needed next.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E2/v1 only after establishing this obligation: The restricted\nstructure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis countable, contains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>,\nand every existential formula true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith parameters in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nhas a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Use finiteness of parameter tuples to locate all their entries in one\nstage.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Closure under functions alone is insufficient for elementarity;\nretain the existential witness closure as a distinct export.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N2.1–N2.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Prove elementarity by induction", "role": "<p>Induct through atomic formulas, Boolean operations, and existential\nquantification in both directions. <strong>Local obligation:</strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\preccurlyeq M</annotation></semantics></math>;\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nis countably infinite.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E2/v1 and recursive first-order semantics. Incoming proof exports:\nE2/v1 at N3.1.</p>", "exportId": "E3", "export": "<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\preccurlyeq M</annotation></semantics></math>;\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nis countably infinite.</p>", "gate": "<p>An existential witness true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nmust be transferred using the induction hypothesis for its matrix, not\nsimply declared true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "repair": "<p>If elementarity is inferred from function closure, add the witness\nproperty and the two directions of the existential induction;\nsubstructure alone does not imply elementary substructure. Withhold this\nexport and recheck its descendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Terms evaluate identically in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\non tuples from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>,\nby induction and function restriction. Atomic equalities and relations\ntherefore agree. If formulas agree, their negations and conjunctions\nagree by the same truth tables.</p>", "<p>For an existential formula, a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nmakes its matrix true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nby the induction hypothesis, proving the forward implication.\nConversely, if the existential formula is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>,\nE2 supplies a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhose matrix is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe induction hypothesis makes that matrix true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus the existential formula is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis completes the induction for all formulas and proves\nelementarity.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite, for every positive integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>\nit satisfies the sentence asserting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>\npairwise distinct elements. Elementarity transfers each such sentence to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite. Since it is at most countable, it is countably\ninfinite.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E3/v1 only after establishing this obligation:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≼</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N\\preccurlyeq M</annotation></semantics></math>;\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nis countably infinite.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E2/v1 and recursive first-order semantics. Incoming proof exports:\nE2/v1 at N3.1. Retain the stated assumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Induct through atomic formulas, Boolean operations, and existential\nquantification in both directions.</p>", "Sense": "<p>An existential witness true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nmust be transferred using the induction hypothesis for its matrix, not\nsimply declared true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N3.1–N3.3 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}], "edges": [{"id": "T1-2", "source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N2.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>⋃</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=\\bigcup_{n\\ge0}B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nCheck: Closure under functions alone is insufficient for elementarity;\nretain the existential witness closure as a distinct export.</p>"}, {"id": "T2-3", "source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "activity": "N3.1", "gate": "<p>E2/v1 and recursive first-order semantics. Check: An existential\nwitness true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nmust be transferred using the induction hypothesis for its matrix, not\nsimply declared true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-build-the-witness-closure-1\">N1 — Build the witness\nclosure</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N1.1.</strong> Choose one element of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nand adjoin it to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>\nto make\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_0</annotation></semantics></math>\nnonempty. Given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\ninclude in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>\nthe interpretations of all constants and of all finite-arity function\nsymbols applied to tuples from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThere are countably many such requests.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N1.2.</strong> Also list every existential formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\,\\psi(x,\\bar b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith a finite tuple\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar b</annotation></semantics></math>\nfrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nthat is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThere are countably many formulas and parameter tuples. Choose one\nwitness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nfor each true request and add it. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>\nitself included, the result remains countable and the construction can\nbe repeated.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-obtain-a-countable-substructure-with-witnesses-1\">N2 — Obtain\na countable substructure with witnesses</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N2.1.</strong> The union\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nis nonempty and at most countable. Any finite tuple from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>\nlies entirely in some\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_n</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso each language function evaluated there lies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_{n+1}\\subset B</annotation></semantics></math>.\nConstants also lie in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus restriction defines an\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L</annotation></semantics></math>-substructure\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\ncontaining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N2.2.</strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊨</mo><mo>∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M\\models\\exists x\\,\\psi(x,\\bar b)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>b</mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾</mo></mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar b</annotation></semantics></math>\nin\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nchoose a stage containing the whole finite tuple. N1 added a witness at\nthe next stage. Hence such a witness is in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwith the matrix formula true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis is the precise witness property needed next.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-prove-elementarity-by-induction-1\">N3 — Prove elementarity by\ninduction</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N3.1.</strong> Terms evaluate identically in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\non tuples from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>,\nby induction and function restriction. Atomic equalities and relations\ntherefore agree. If formulas agree, their negations and conjunctions\nagree by the same truth tables.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N3.2.</strong> For an existential formula, a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nmakes its matrix true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nby the induction hypothesis, proving the forward implication.\nConversely, if the existential formula is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>,\nE2 supplies a witness in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhose matrix is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>;\nthe induction hypothesis makes that matrix true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThus the existential formula is true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThis completes the induction for all formulas and proves\nelementarity.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N3.3.</strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite, for every positive integer\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>\nit satisfies the sentence asserting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r</annotation></semantics></math>\npairwise distinct elements. Elementarity transfers each such sentence to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N</annotation></semantics></math>\nis infinite. Since it is at most countable, it is countably\ninfinite.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>Apply the construction to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>+</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>⋅</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mathbb R,+,\\cdot,&lt;,0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith seed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=\\mathbb Q</annotation></semantics></math>.\nThe true formula\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>∧</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\,(x&gt;0\\wedge x^2=2)</annotation></semantics></math>\ndemands a witness, so the closure adds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt2</annotation></semantics></math>.\nRepeated stages supply witnesses for all formulas over the growing set.\nThe resulting countable elementary substructure is more than the initial\nrational field; the construction does not assert that it equals a\nparticular named subfield.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>The subfield\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Q\\subset\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math>\nis a substructure of ordered fields but not an elementary substructure,\nbecause\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\,(x^2=2)</annotation></semantics></math>\nis false in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Q</annotation></semantics></math>\nand true in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℝ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb R</annotation></semantics></math>.\nCountability of the language also matters: if a distinct constant names\nevery element of an uncountable structure, every substructure must\ncontain all those named elements.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Rejected argument, diagnosis, and correction", "html": "<p><strong>Rejected N2 conclusion:</strong> closing the seed under field\noperations is enough. Starting with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Q</annotation></semantics></math>\nleaves\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb Q</annotation></semantics></math>,\nwhich fails the square-root existential sentence.\n<strong>Diagnosis:</strong> Memory recorded function closure but omitted\nexistential witnesses. <strong>Repair:</strong> add one witness for\nevery true existential request at every stage, then prove formula\nagreement by N3. The original theorem and seed are preserved; recheck\ncountability and the elementarity induction.</p>"}, {"label": "Publication and reuse", "html": "<p>The corrected export retains its explicit assumptions. A hypothetical\nrevision receives a new version, and each consuming activity is\nrechecked; this scenario is not a recorded runtime event. <strong>Next\ndirection:</strong> Explore the distinction between internal and\nexternal countability in models of set theory, without interpreting the\ntheorem as a contradiction about cardinality.</p>"}], "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes_4.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Set Theory notes</a> for sets and ultraproducts; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~antonio/math125A/mathlogicP1.pdf\">Berkeley,\nMathematical Logic for Mathematicians, Part I</a> for first-order\nsemantics; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/computability_notes_v1.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Computability Theory notes</a> for effective procedures and\nundecidability. The proofs below specify their own set-theoretic and\nsemantic assumptions.</p>", "sha256": "1e843cef04a566be763917086f8f2d6e80d1386bccb04543b18c7f73c5891448", "pdf": "reports/pdf/downward-lowenheim-skolem.pdf"}, {"id": "LF-05", "slug": "halting-problem-undecidability", "title": "Undecidability of the Halting Problem", "level": "Intermediate", "idea": "<p>Assuming a decider allows construction of a machine that reverses its\nanswer on the diagonal input. The contradiction concerns a total\ndecision procedure; direct simulation still provides a one-sided\nrecognition procedure.</p>", "target": "<p>Fix an effective encoding of deterministic Turing machines by finite\nstrings. Prove no total computable function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(e,x)\\in\\{0,1\\}</annotation></semantics></math>\ncorrectly decides for every valid machine code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e</annotation></semantics></math>\nand input string\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>\nwhether that machine eventually halts on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>.\nAlso prove the halting set is computably enumerable: there is a\nprocedure that halts and accepts exactly the halting pairs. Invalid\ncodes can be assigned a fixed decidable convention and do not affect the\nresult.</p>", "imports": ["<p>Use a universal step-by-step simulator for the fixed Turing-machine\nmodel, effective composition and conditional branching of finite\nprograms, and the ability to implement an infinite loop. Every such\nfinite program has an encoding in the same model. These are elementary\nproperties of the computational model, not an oracle or a solution of\nthe target decision problem.</p>"], "nodes": [{"id": "N1", "title": "Compile the diagonal adversary", "role": "<p>Construct a finite machine that consults\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand then deliberately does the opposite. <strong>Local\nobligation:</strong> There is a machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith a valid code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(z)</annotation></semantics></math>\nloops if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand halts if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "inputs": "<p>A hypothetical total computable correct decider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "exportId": "E1", "export": "<p>There is a machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith a valid code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(z)</annotation></semantics></math>\nloops if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand halts if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "gate": "<p>Totality makes the branch selection terminate; the constructed\nmachine may itself loop, which is permitted for an ordinary machine.</p>", "repair": "<p>If the argument assumes a possibly nonterminating test before\nbranching, restore the total-decider hypothesis; without it the reversal\nwould not be implemented on every input. Withhold this export and\nrecheck its descendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>Define a program on input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math>:\ncompute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nif the result is one, execute an infinite loop; if the result is zero,\nhalt. Under the hypothesis, the computation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>\nalways finishes and this is an effective finite program.</p>", "<p>By the fixed model’s effective composition and encoding, this program\nis a Turing machine with some valid code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>.\nGiving a program its own code as an input is legal: codes are finite\nstrings and machines accept finite strings. No machine needs to discover\nits own code internally.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E1/v1 only after establishing this obligation: There is a\nmachine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>\nwith a valid code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>\nsuch that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(z)</annotation></semantics></math>\nloops if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)=1</annotation></semantics></math>\nand halts if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Memory": "<p>A hypothetical total computable correct decider\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>.\nRetain the stated assumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Construct a finite machine that consults\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)</annotation></semantics></math>\nand then deliberately does the opposite.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Totality makes the branch selection terminate; the constructed\nmachine may itself loop, which is permitted for an ordinary machine.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N1.1–N1.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N2", "title": "Contradict the decision at its own code", "role": "<p>Exhaust both possible Boolean answers and compare them with the\nconstructed behavior. <strong>Local obligation:</strong> No total\ncomputable halting decider exists.</p>", "inputs": "<p>E1/v1 and the purported correctness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>\non the valid pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(d,d)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1.</p>", "exportId": "E2", "export": "<p>No total computable halting decider exists.</p>", "gate": "<p>The contradiction is to the existence of a correct total\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>,\nnot to the existence of the perfectly valid looping machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "repair": "<p>If only one answer branch is analyzed, check the other explicitly;\nundecidability requires defeating every possible returned answer.\nWithhold this export and recheck its descendants after a versioned\ncorrection.</p>", "activities": ["<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(d,d)=1</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncorrectness predicts that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(d)</annotation></semantics></math>\nhalts. But by construction that branch loops forever, a\ncontradiction.</p>", "<p>If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(d,d)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncorrectness predicts that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(d)</annotation></semantics></math>\nnever halts. But the other branch halts immediately after the completed\ncall to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>,\nalso a contradiction. These exhaust the two outputs, so the supposed\ndecider cannot exist.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E2/v1 only after establishing this obligation: No total\ncomputable halting decider exists.</p>", "Memory": "<p>E1/v1 and the purported correctness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>\non the valid pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(d,d)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nIncoming proof exports: E1/v1 at N2.1. Retain the stated assumptions and\nimport versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Exhaust both possible Boolean answers and compare them with the\nconstructed behavior.</p>", "Sense": "<p>The contradiction is to the existence of a correct total\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>,\nnot to the existence of the perfectly valid looping machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N2.1–N2.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}, {"id": "N3", "title": "Separate recognition from decision", "role": "<p>Simulate one step at a time and accept when a halt is observed.\n<strong>Local obligation:</strong> The halting set is computably\nenumerable but not decidable.</p>", "inputs": "<p>A valid encoded machine-input pair and the universal simulator; E2/v1\nfor the distinction from decision. Incoming proof exports: E2/v1 at\nN3.2.</p>", "exportId": "E3", "export": "<p>The halting set is computably enumerable but not decidable.</p>", "gate": "<p>Failure to observe a halt by a finite time does not certify\nnonhalting.</p>", "repair": "<p>If simulation is presented as a decision procedure, state its\nnonterminating behavior on negative instances and keep the\nrecognition/decision distinction. Withhold this export and recheck its\ndescendants after a versioned correction.</p>", "activities": ["<p>On input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(e,x)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsimulate the machine coded by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>\none step at a time. If the simulated machine reaches a halting state,\naccept. If it never halts, the simulator continues forever. Thus the\nprocedure accepts exactly the halting pairs.</p>", "<p>This is a recognizer, not a decider, because nonhalting inputs need\nnot return an answer. In conjunction with E2, it proves that the halting\nset is computably enumerable and undecidable.</p>"], "faculties": {"Ego": "<p>Publish E3/v1 only after establishing this obligation: The halting\nset is computably enumerable but not decidable.</p>", "Memory": "<p>A valid encoded machine-input pair and the universal simulator; E2/v1\nfor the distinction from decision. Incoming proof exports: E2/v1 at\nN3.2. Retain the stated assumptions and import versions.</p>", "Intellect": "<p>Simulate one step at a time and accept when a halt is observed.</p>", "Sense": "<p>Failure to observe a halt by a finite time does not certify\nnonhalting.</p>", "Action": "<p>Carry out activities N3.1–N3.2 below, retaining each calculation and\nconstruction as evidence.</p>"}}], "edges": [{"id": "T1-2", "source": "N1", "target": "N2", "export": "E1/v1", "activity": "N2.1", "gate": "<p>E1/v1 and the purported correctness of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>\non the valid pair\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(d,d)</annotation></semantics></math>.\nCheck: The contradiction is to the existence of a correct total\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>,\nnot to the existence of the perfectly valid looping machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>"}, {"id": "T2-3", "source": "N2", "target": "N3", "export": "E2/v1", "activity": "N3.2", "gate": "<p>A valid encoded machine-input pair and the universal simulator; E2/v1\nfor the distinction from decision. Check: Failure to observe a halt by a\nfinite time does not certify nonhalting.</p>"}], "proof": "<h3 id=\"n1-compile-the-diagonal-adversary-1\">N1 — Compile the diagonal\nadversary</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N1.1.</strong> Define a program on input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z</annotation></semantics></math>:\ncompute\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(z,z)</annotation></semantics></math>;\nif the result is one, execute an infinite loop; if the result is zero,\nhalt. Under the hypothesis, the computation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>\nalways finishes and this is an effective finite program.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N1.2.</strong> By the fixed model’s effective composition\nand encoding, this program is a Turing machine with some valid code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d</annotation></semantics></math>.\nGiving a program its own code as an input is legal: codes are finite\nstrings and machines accept finite strings. No machine needs to discover\nits own code internally.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n2-contradict-the-decision-at-its-own-code-1\">N2 — Contradict\nthe decision at its own code</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N2.1.</strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(d,d)=1</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncorrectness predicts that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(d)</annotation></semantics></math>\nhalts. But by construction that branch loops forever, a\ncontradiction.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N2.2.</strong> If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(d,d)=0</annotation></semantics></math>,\ncorrectness predicts that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(d)</annotation></semantics></math>\nnever halts. But the other branch halts immediately after the completed\ncall to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H</annotation></semantics></math>,\nalso a contradiction. These exhaust the two outputs, so the supposed\ndecider cannot exist.</p></li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"n3-separate-recognition-from-decision-1\">N3 — Separate\nrecognition from decision</h3>\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li><p><strong>N3.1.</strong> On input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(e,x)</annotation></semantics></math>,\nsimulate the machine coded by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e</annotation></semantics></math>\non\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math>\none step at a time. If the simulated machine reaches a halting state,\naccept. If it never halts, the simulator continues forever. Thus the\nprocedure accepts exactly the halting pairs.</p></li>\n<li><p><strong>N3.2.</strong> This is a recognizer, not a decider,\nbecause nonhalting inputs need not return an answer. In conjunction with\nE2, it proves that the halting set is computably enumerable and\nundecidable.</p></li>\n</ol>", "example": "<p>A machine that immediately halts on any input is accepted by the\nsimulator after finitely many steps. A machine with a single transition\nback to the same nonhalting state is simulated indefinitely. If a\nhypothetical decider labels the diagonal machine\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(d)</annotation></semantics></math>\nas halting, its prescribed branch is the loop; if it labels it\nnonhalting, its branch halts. The contradiction is symbolic and does not\nclaim that such a decider was run.</p>", "boundary": ["<p>The theorem does not say no individual nonhalting behavior can be\nproved. The one-state self-loop is easy to certify, and any fixed finite\ncollection of known examples can be classified. What is impossible is\none total computable procedure that is correct on every machine-input\npair in the stated unbounded model.</p>"], "repair": [{"label": "Rejected argument, diagnosis, and correction", "html": "<p><strong>Rejected N3 rule:</strong> after a million simulated steps\nwithout halting, return “never halts.” A finite program can count to a\nmillion plus one and then halt. <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> Sense\nidentifies finite observation being promoted to an unbounded universal\nclaim. <strong>Repair:</strong> let the recognizer keep running on\nunresolved inputs; it remains a valid semidecision procedure but no\nlonger claims totality. This is an explicit weakening of the rejected\nalgorithmic claim, consistent with the proved impossibility theorem.</p>"}, {"label": "Publication and reuse", "html": "<p>The corrected export retains its explicit assumptions. A hypothetical\nrevision receives a new version, and each consuming activity is\nrechecked; this scenario is not a recorded runtime event. <strong>Next\ndirection:</strong> Develop many-one reductions to transfer\nundecidability to other decision problems, recording the computability\nand correctness of each reduction.</p>"}], "references": "<p><a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/set_theory_notes_4.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Set Theory notes</a> for sets and ultraproducts; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~antonio/math125A/mathlogicP1.pdf\">Berkeley,\nMathematical Logic for Mathematicians, Part I</a> for first-order\nsemantics; <a\nhref=\"https://math.berkeley.edu/~marks/notes/computability_notes_v1.pdf\">Andrew\nMarks, Computability Theory notes</a> for effective procedures and\nundecidability. The proofs below specify their own set-theoretic and\nsemantic assumptions.</p>", "sha256": "87b489852e7b25386fe87c6738af4dbbfe7f0de575ed0621d4bda9dbf8d925d4", "pdf": "reports/pdf/halting-problem-undecidability.pdf"}], "links": [], "evidence": "Authored proof walkthroughs relative to declared imports; no agent execution or formal verification claimed."};
